/*
    town
    ---------------------------

    JaCaMo Application File

    A small-town economy used to study prices, supply, demand, inflation,
    taxes, employment and market power.

    HOW TO READ THE PARAMETERS BELOW - they are the experiment, not
    boilerplate. Change one at a time and re-run; the timekeeper prints a
    per-round line so you can compare the two economies honestly.

      firm     unit_cost
                       unit cost. Higher cost means a higher floor, so
                       this firm stops undercutting earlier and ends up
                       with a thinner slice of the market.
      firm     capacity units available per round. This is the scarcity
                       lever: shrink total capacity below total demand and
                       rationing appears as `unmet` in the report.
      house    cash     endowment. A household that runs out drops out of
                       the market permanently - it has no income yet.
      house    reservation
                       the most this household will pay. This spread,
                       and nothing else, is the demand curve.

    The starting price of 14.0 is set well above every firm's cost on
    purpose, so that competition pushes the price down through the
    households' reservation prices and you can watch demand grow as price
    falls.
*/

mas town {

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Supply side: three firms, three costs.
    //
    // A firm pays out, to households, its LABOR cost (unit_cost per unit
    // sold), its OVERHEAD (fixed, every round, whether or not it sold), and a
    // DIVIDEND of wage_share * profit. Wages are NOT a share of revenue: that
    // would never charge the firm for making the goods, and money would be
    // conserved while the firm quietly drained the households' endowments.
    // Pay production cost first, dividend out of what is left.
    //
// overhead is what makes being OPEN cost something, which is the entire
// basis of firm exit. Under Bertrand the price pins at
// `cheapest cost + 0.5`, so late in a run firm_a earns only 0.5 per unit at
// cost 7. Overhead is therefore the knife edge between survival and death:
// early on, while prices start at 14, firm_a earns ~6.5 per sale and the
// expensive firms earn 5.5 and 4.5, so all three bank reserves. Once the
// price has collapsed, firm_a keeps clearing at cost + 0.5 while firm_b and
// firm_c are undercut out of the market entirely and earn nothing above a
// fixed bill they cannot stop paying. Overhead 1.2 is calibrated so firm_a
// out-earns its rent and the other two out-run theirs. Try overhead(0.6) to
// watch nobody ever leave, or overhead(2.0) to watch the efficient firm die
// too - which is the point: Bertrand competition has no equilibrium that lets
// a firm rest easy, which is precisely why firms have an incentive to collude.
    //
    // wage_share is the dividend lever: 0.0 retains all profit as capital,
    // 1.0 hands every unit of surplus to households and firms never
    // accumulate anything.
    //
    // Firms focus the ledger as well as the market, and open at zero, so they
    // are capitalised purely by what they sell.
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    agent firm_a : firm.asl {
        beliefs: unit_cost(7.0), capacity(1), overhead(0.5), wage_share(0.5)
        focus:   w.mkt
                 w.led
        roles:   producer in towngrp
    }

    agent firm_b : firm.asl {
        beliefs: unit_cost(8.0), capacity(1), overhead(0.5), wage_share(0.5)
        focus:   w.mkt
                 w.led
        roles:   producer in towngrp
    }

    agent firm_c : firm.asl {
        beliefs: unit_cost(9.0), capacity(1), overhead(0.5), wage_share(0.5)
        focus:   w.mkt
                 w.led
        roles:   producer in towngrp
    }

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Demand side: six households, one endowment, a spread of limits.
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    agent h1 : household.asl {
        beliefs: cash(60.0), reservation(14.0)
        focus:   w.mkt
                 w.led
        roles:   resident in towngrp
    }

    agent h2 : household.asl {
        beliefs: cash(60.0), reservation(13.0)
        focus:   w.mkt
                 w.led
        roles:   resident in towngrp
    }

    agent h3 : household.asl {
        beliefs: cash(60.0), reservation(12.0)
        focus:   w.mkt
                 w.led
        roles:   resident in towngrp
    }

    agent h4 : household.asl {
        beliefs: cash(60.0), reservation(11.0)
        focus:   w.mkt
                 w.led
        roles:   resident in towngrp
    }

    agent h5 : household.asl {
        beliefs: cash(60.0), reservation(9.0)
        focus:   w.mkt
                 w.led
        roles:   resident in towngrp
    }

    agent h6 : household.asl {
        beliefs: cash(60.0), reservation(8.0)
        focus:   w.mkt
                 w.led
        roles:   resident in towngrp
    }

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // The clock. Not a member of the town: it owns time, nothing else.
    // max_rounds is how long the town runs before it closes its books.
    // Set it to 200 to watch the money drain out of the households, or to
    // 15 to stop while price is still falling.
    //
    // Keep this block shaped exactly like the household blocks above -
    // beliefs, then focus, no blank lines and no comments inside. The jcm
    // parser is not as forgiving as it looks, and a block it cannot read
    // silently produces an agent that never focuses anything.
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // The clock is a resident like everyone else, it simply never trades.
    // Giving it the same `roles:` line as the firms and households is not
    // cosmetic: an agent block with focus but no roles did not get focused
    // at all, and the launcher said nothing about it.
    agent timekeeper : timekeeper.asl {
        beliefs: max_rounds(30)
        focus:   w.mkt
                 w.led
                 w.dash
        roles:   coordinator in towngrp
    }

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // The shared environment.
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    workspace w {
        artifact mkt: town.Market(14.0)
        artifact led: town.Ledger()
        artifact dash: town.TownDashboard(30)
    }

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // The institution. See src/org/town-os.xml.
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    organisation townorg : town-os.xml {
        group towngrp: townGroup {
            players: firm_a producer
                     firm_b producer
                     firm_c producer
                     h1     resident
                     h2     resident
                     h3     resident
                     h4     resident
                     h5     resident
                     h6     resident
        }
        scheme trade: trade {
            debug: inspector_gui(on)
        }
    }

}
